:::figure TDCSGFigures.criticalChord (label := "critical-chord") (inline := true) (links := TDCSGFigures.criticalChord_spec) (alt := "Two closed disks of radius the square root of three plus phi, centered at minus one and one, drawn as circles, with their overlap shaded. The chord from minus E on the right circle to E on the left circle passes through zero in three colored pieces numbered 1, 2 and 3. The circles cross at zeta minus zeta to the fourth and its negative.")
The two closed disks at the critical radius $`R=\sqrt{3+\varphi}\approx2.149`, centered at $`-1`
(labelled left disk) and $`1` (labelled right disk), and the lens they share. The chord from
$`-E` to $`E`, where $`E=\zeta-\zeta^2` and $`\zeta=\exp(2\pi i/5)`, passes through $`0`; its
pieces 1, 2 and 3 are the ones the three words of Chapter 3 move. The point $`E` lies on the left
circle, since $`|E+1|^2=3+\varphi`, and $`-E` on the right circle; the circles cross at
$`\pm(\zeta-\zeta^4)`. Every point is exact in $`\mathbb{Z}[\zeta]` and the theorem under the
picture states what it draws.
:::

Place two closed disks of radius $`r` in the plane, centered at $`-1` and $`1`. One move turns
the left disk clockwise through a fifth of a full turn, carrying the points inside it along and
leaving every point outside it where it is; the other move does the same with the right disk.
Let $`GG_5(r)` be the group of permutations of the plane generated by these two moves, and let
$`\varphi=(1+\sqrt5)/2` be the golden ratio.

*Main theorem.* For every real number $`r`, the group $`GG_5(r)` is finite if and only if
$`r<\sqrt{3+\varphi}`. In particular the critical radius of fivefold rotations is exactly
$`\sqrt{3+\varphi}\approx2.149`, and at the critical radius itself the group is already infinite.

The theorem is checked in Lean, with Mathlib, as
`TDCSG.CriticalRadius.finite_GG_five_iff` and `TDCSG.CriticalRadius.criticalRadius_five`. This
site is a blueprint of that proof: a written proof whose every numbered statement is linked to the
Lean declarations that prove it.

*Why it is true.* The proof has two halves, which meet at the threshold.

* *At and above the threshold the group is infinite.* {figref "critical-chord"}[] shows a chord
  through zero whose ends lie on the boundary circles exactly when $`r=\sqrt{3+\varphi}`. Three fixed
  sequences of moves each slide a piece of this chord along itself, and together they move points
  of the chord like a rotation of a circle by an irrational angle. Starting from zero, they reach
  infinitely many different points.
* *Below the threshold the group is finite.* Follow a point through any sequence of moves and
  record which disk centers act on it. These centers can be labelled by integer vectors, and the
  sequence of moves becomes a walk in a graph on the labels. Below the threshold, an estimate on
  the overlap of two disks leaves, in every coordinate of the labels, levels that a walk can cross
  only by stepping onto a dead end and back. So every walk stays in a box whose size does not
  depend on the starting point, every orbit has a bounded number of points, and the group is
  finite.

:::figure_explorer TDCSGFigures.orbitRadius (label := "orbit-radius") (alt := "Two closed disks about minus one and one whose radius grows from 1.2 to the critical radius, with the orbit of zero drawn as dots: a few points for small radii, more and more near the critical radius.")
The orbit of $`0` under the two moves as the radius $`r` grows, computed exactly in
$`\mathbb{Z}[\zeta]` at each radius. Below the critical radius it is finite: 9 points at $`r=1.2`
and $`r=1.9`, then 71 and 151 points at $`r=1.95` and $`2.05` (and 801 at $`r=2.1`, not drawn).
At $`r=R` it is infinite; the last frame shows only its first 500 points, in the order a
breadth-first search finds them.
:::

*How to read it.* Chapters 1 and 2 set up the objects and state the theorem; they also introduce
the Lean notation for readers new to Lean.

* {ref "compound-symmetry"}[Chapter 1, Compound symmetry]: the general construction behind the
  groups, as defined in the Lean library, and its general properties.
* {ref "disks"}[Chapter 2, Two disks and the critical radius]: the groups $`GG_n(r)`, the
  critical radius, and the statement of the main theorem.
* {ref "part-infinite"}[Part I, Infinite at the threshold]: {ref "chord"}[Chapter 3, The chord
  and its three words].
* {ref "part-finite"}[Part II, Finite below the threshold]: {ref "lift"}[Chapter 4, Lift to
  integer labels], {ref "lens"}[Chapter 5, The lens estimate], {ref "levels"}[Chapter 6, Good
  levels] and {ref "confinement"}[Chapter 7, Confinement].
* {ref "main-theorem"}[Chapter 8, Proof of the main theorem]: the two halves joined.
* {ref "other-orders"}[Chapter 9, Other orders]: orders 8, 10 and 12, what the library proves
  for them and what is open.
* {ref "verification"}[Chapter 10, Verification]: the independent statement of the theorem and
  how it is checked.

Each numbered statement opens beside the Lean declarations it describes. After the chapters come
four pages. {ref "figure-theorems"}[Figures and their theorems] gives, for each figure, the Lean
theorem that states what the figure draws; the dependency graph shows how the numbered statements
depend on each other; the summary lists the statements and their status; and the page Figures
lists every figure of the site with its caption, the declaration that draws it and its theorem.

*How to check it.* A short challenge file states the theorem using Mathlib alone, with its own
definitions of the two moves and of the number $`\sqrt{3+(1+\sqrt5)/2}`. Lake Comparator checks
that the library proves exactly this statement and replays the proof with the Lean kernel,
allowing only the standard axioms. Chapter 10 describes the check and how to run it, and every
published version of this site links its commit to the run that checked it.

The groups were introduced, and the fivefold closed form proposed, in the
[original paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.12950). The
{repoFile "notes/proofs/sharp-lower-bound-n5-pentagrid.md"}[integer-coordinate proof]
is the written proof of the finite half.

